## B. Give-up regime and cost of a mis-scaled interval

dR/dh at h=0+ equals 2 f_eps(0) > 0 for every density with f(0)>0, so a zero-width
interval is NEVER optimal; as s/sig -> inf the optimum tends to h* = sig/2 and
R* -> f_eps(0)*sig/e (flat-density limit).  Check (gauss):
  k=   2: h*/sig=0.4901  R*=0.07264  f(0)*sig/e=0.07338
  k=   5: h*/sig=0.4983  R*=0.02930  f(0)*sig/e=0.02935
  k=  20: h*/sig=0.4999  R*=0.00734  f(0)*sig/e=0.00734
  k= 100: h*/sig=0.5000  R*=0.00147  f(0)*sig/e=0.00147

Reward ratio R(h fitted for scale m*s) / R*(s): using a half-width calibrated to the wrong scale (m<1: calibrated on errors that are smaller than the truth, i.e. local residuals when live is inflated by 1/m).

